From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jaime Dias <jaimejosediasnt@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 19 Aug 2026 00:20:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 32/32] fbgfx: let the boot splash hold the display until the
 desktop is drawn

m68k-emu68 draws a boot splash straight into the linear framebuffer before
graphics.library exists. There is one framebuffer and no way for two producers
to share it, so the splash keeps ownership until a display is about to be
installed and gives it up in the same breath -- which is what that port's own
driver has always done, in the Show() of arch/m68k-emu68/hidd/emu68gfx.

Building fbgfx for that target instead dropped the hand-off silently: the
splash simply stopped being drawn on when Wanderer opened over it, with nothing
in the log to say the transfer never happened. Restoring it here is what lets
the driver be swapped without the behaviour changing.

Before the lock and before anything is made visible, because the splash writes
to the framebuffer without taking it -- stopping it after the bitmap is shown
would leave a window with both drawing.

It also has to stop clearing. initFBGfxHW() ends with ClearBuffer(), a memset
of the whole surface at driver init -- long before the hand-off -- which wipes
the splash artwork while the boot is still running. Only the artwork: the
progress band is repainted on every stage change and comes back, so the symptom
is a loader floating on black rather than a blank screen, which is what the
user reported. The aarch64 path keeps the clear because by that point it has
already detached its framebuffer console and nothing else owns the surface.

Guarded on __EMU68__ rather than abstracted. What a bootstrap has to be told
differs per target, and this file already carries the aarch64 equivalent in
initFBGfxHW(): RawPutChar(0x03) to detach the framebuffer console. A hook
generalising two unrelated one-liners would cost more than it saves, and the
guard keeps the aarch64 build byte-identical.

Second revision: the hand-off became a *hold*.

Taking the framebuffer at the first Show() is right for a driver that composes
straight into it. This one does not -- it refreshes from each bitmap's own
buffer through fbDoRefreshArea() -- so suppressing that copy leaves the splash
on screen while Intuition and Wanderer assemble the desktop behind it. That is
double buffering with the bitmap as the back buffer, at no extra cost, and it
is what makes "keep the splash until the icons are up" cheap here when it was
not with a driver that drew into the framebuffer directly.

The hold ends on BOOTUI_STAGE_ICONS or on its own deadline, whichever comes
first, and the release callback repaints the whole screen in one go -- nothing
has reached the framebuffer by then. Re-asserting aHidd_BitMap_Visible would
not do it: the bitmap class only refreshes from Set() when the scroll offsets
changed.

The deadline is not a detail to tidy away later. Nothing is yet in a position
to send the ICONS stage -- the Startup-Sequence blocks on WANDERER:Wanderer, so
a line after it runs when Wanderer exits -- and a hold that never ends is a
machine showing a frozen splash with a working desktop invisible behind it. So
the deadline always applies, and the signal, when something can send it, only
makes the release prompt.

Third revision: the repaint runs from the task that signals, not from a timer.

Two delayed variants were tried and both failed for the same reason -- the
repaint takes a semaphore and walks OOP objects, so it can only run from a
task, and no task of ours comes past on its own.

Calling it from the timer that ends the hold earned two recoverable alerts:

    ObtainSemaphore called in supervisor mode!!!
    sem = 0x0209d6ec task = 0x0209d0d0 (WANDERER:Wanderer)

Deferring it to the next fbDoRefreshArea() removed the alerts and replaced them
with a seventy-second gap, measured: the hold released at 1:05 and the screen
changed at 2:15, because once the icons are drawn nothing draws again for a
long time. Waiting for the drawing to go quiet is worse still -- it releases
precisely when nothing is left to carry the repaint.

So the driver registers the repaint with the splash, and the splash calls it
from BOOTUI_STAGE_ICONS, which arrives on Wanderer's own task with the icons
already in the bitmap. One moment that is both "the desktop is ready" and "we
are on a task".

Fourth revision: the hold is scoped to the first screen.

It exists to cover the gap between the Workbench screen appearing and Wanderer
drawing its icons, so engaging on the first Show -- which at boot is that
screen -- is right, and staying engaged across later ones is not. Anything that
opens a screen afterwards has a better claim on the display than a splash, so a
subsequent Show ends the hold rather than being held behind it.

Both paths run on a task: Show() executes on whoever opened the screen, which
is what makes calling the release from there legal where calling it from a
timer is not.

---

Reworked 2026-08-22: the driver no longer decides the boot lifecycle. It
registers the means -- a callback that puts the finished screen up -- and
nothing else. Whether anything is held, and until when, is decided from
the presentation boundary graphics.library reports above every driver,
because a driver deciding when a splash ends is the dependency that has
to go. What stays here is genuinely this driver's own business: it
refreshes from each bitmap's own buffer, so not taking the framebuffer at
the first Show() is a property of how it composes, not a favour to
anything.

--- a/arch/aarch64-raspi/hidd/fbgfx/fbgfx_support.c
+++ b/arch/aarch64-raspi/hidd/fbgfx/fbgfx_support.c
@@ -22,6 +22,9 @@
 
 #include "fbgfx_intern.h"
 #include "fbgfx_hidd.h"
+#ifdef __EMU68__
+#include <boot.h>
+#endif
 
 BOOL initFBGfxHW(struct HWData *data)
 {
@@ -119,7 +122,21 @@
         data->framebuffer, data->bytesperline,
         data->swappixelbytes ? ", byte-swapped" : "");
 
+#ifdef __EMU68__
+    /*
+     * Not here on this target. m68k-emu68's bootstrap is still drawing its
+     * splash on this surface and does not give it up until the first Show()
+     * (see the __EMU68__ block in fbgfx_displayclass.c), so clearing at driver
+     * init wipes the artwork while the boot is still running -- and only the
+     * artwork, because the progress band is repainted on every stage change
+     * and comes back. The symptom is a loader floating on black.
+     *
+     * The aarch64 path clears because it has already detached its framebuffer
+     * console above, so by this point nothing else owns the surface.
+     */
+#else
     ClearBuffer(data);
+#endif
     return TRUE;
 }
 
@@ -131,6 +148,31 @@
     ULONG srcmod, dstmod;
     LONG y, w, h, sx, sy;
 
+#ifdef __EMU68__
+    /*
+     * The boot splash still owns the framebuffer. Everything drawn now lands
+     * in the bitmap's own buffer and stays there.
+     */
+    if (emu68_bootui_holding())
+        return;
+
+    /*
+     * The hold has just ended and nothing of the desktop has reached the
+     * framebuffer, so the first refresh after it has to be the whole screen
+     * rather than whatever rectangle the caller asked for.
+     *
+     * Collected here rather than pushed from the timer that ends the hold:
+     * that runs in an interrupt, where taking the framebuffer semaphore is
+     * illegal -- the version that did it earned two "called in supervisor
+     * mode" alerts from Wanderer.
+     */
+    if (emu68_bootui_take_release())
+    {
+        x1 = 0; y1 = 0;
+        x2 = data->width; y2 = data->height;
+    }
+#endif
+
     x1 += data->xoffset; y1 += data->yoffset;
     x2 += data->xoffset; y2 += data->yoffset;
 
--- a/arch/aarch64-raspi/hidd/fbgfx/fbgfx_displayclass.c
+++ b/arch/aarch64-raspi/hidd/fbgfx/fbgfx_displayclass.c
@@ -30,6 +30,53 @@
 #include "fbgfx_hidd.h"
 #include "fbgfx_support.h"
 
+#ifdef __EMU68__
+/*
+ * m68k-emu68 draws a boot splash straight into the framebuffer before
+ * graphics.library exists, and has to be told to stop.
+ *
+ * There is one linear framebuffer and no way for two producers to share it, so
+ * the splash keeps ownership until the display is about to be installed and
+ * gives it up in the same breath. This is the same hand-off the port's own
+ * driver performs -- see the comment above
+ * Emu68Display__Hidd_Display__Show() in arch/m68k-emu68/hidd/emu68gfx -- moved
+ * here so that swapping drivers does not silently drop it.
+ *
+ * Guarded rather than made generic on purpose: what a bootstrap has to be told
+ * differs per target, and this file's own initFBGfxHW() shows the aarch64
+ * equivalent (RawPutChar(0x03), detaching the framebuffer console). A hook
+ * abstracting two unrelated one-liners would cost more than it saved.
+ */
+#include <boot.h>
+#include "fbgfx_intern.h"
+#include "fbgfx_bitmap.h"
+
+/*
+ * Puts the finished desktop up when the splash lets go.
+ *
+ * Nothing of the desktop has reached the framebuffer while the hold was on, so
+ * this is a full-screen copy rather than a rectangle. Called from
+ * emu68_bootui_set_stage() -- Wanderer's task -- so taking the framebuffer
+ * semaphore and walking OOP objects here is legal, which it would not be from
+ * the timer that also can end the hold.
+ */
+static struct FBGfx_staticdata *fbgfx_held_sd;
+
+static void fbgfx_bootui_released(void)
+{
+    struct FBGfx_staticdata *sd = fbgfx_held_sd;
+    struct FBGfxBitMapData *bmdata;
+
+    if (!sd || !sd->visible)
+        return;
+
+    bmdata = OOP_INST_DATA(sd->bmclass, sd->visible);
+    LOCK_FRAMEBUFFER(sd);
+    fbDoRefreshArea(&sd->data, bmdata, 0, 0, bmdata->width, bmdata->height);
+    UNLOCK_FRAMEBUFFER(sd);
+}
+#endif
+
 #include LC_LIBDEFS_FILE
 
 OOP_Object *FBGfxDisplay__Root__New(OOP_Class *cl, OOP_Object *o, struct pRoot_New *msg)
@@ -199,6 +246,30 @@
 
     D(bug("[FBGfx:Display] Show(0x%p), old visible 0x%p\n", msg->bitMap, data->visible));
 
+#ifdef __EMU68__
+    /*
+     * Do not take the framebuffer here -- offer to hold it.
+     *
+     * Taking it at the first Show() is correct for a driver that composes
+     * straight into the framebuffer, and this one does not: it refreshes from
+     * the bitmap's own buffer. So a boot presentation can stay up while the
+     * desktop is built behind it, and the hand-off becomes one repaint of a
+     * finished screen instead of several seconds of half-drawn Workbench.
+     *
+     * What is registered here is the means, not the policy. Whether anything
+     * is held, and for how long, is decided on the other side, from the
+     * presentation boundary graphics.library reports above every driver -- a
+     * driver deciding a boot lifecycle is the dependency this arrangement
+     * exists to avoid. All this says is "if you want to hold, here is how to
+     * put the result up when you are done".
+     */
+    if (msg->bitMap)
+    {
+        fbgfx_held_sd = data;
+        emu68_bootui_set_release_hook(fbgfx_bootui_released);
+    }
+#endif
+
     LOCK_FRAMEBUFFER(data);
 
     /* Remove old bitmap from the screen */
